Thanks! The error message I’m getting is “Cannot read property 'map' of undefined.” I'm trying to render a list of items fetched from an API. The data is stored in state, but it seems like it's not initialized correctly. Here's a snippet of my code:

```javascript
const [items, setItems] = useState();

useEffect(() => {
  fetch('/api/items')
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => setItems(data));
}, []);
```

I thought about initializing `items` as an empty array. Do you think that would fix it?

Yes, that should work! Initializing `items` as an empty array will ensure that it's defined before trying to call the `map` method on it. Here's the updated code:

```javascript
const [items, setItems] = useState([]);

useEffect(() => {
  fetch('/api/items')
   .then(response => response.json())
   .then(data => setItems(data));
}, []);
```

Now, when you try to render the list using `items.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)`, it should work without any issues.

You can add error handling by using a separate state for errors. This will allow you to track and display any errors that occur during the fetch request. Here's an updated version of your code with error handling:

```javascript
const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
const [error, setError] = useState(null);

useEffect(() => {
  fetch('/api/items')
   .then(response => {
      if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
      }
      return response.json();
    })
   .then(data => setItems(data))
   .catch(error => setError(error.message));
}, []);
```

Now, you can display the error message in your UI by checking if `error` is not `null`:

```javascript
{error && <p>Error: {error}</p>}
```

This way, you can track and display any errors that occur during the fetch request, and provide a better user experience by informing the user of any issues.

It's a good practice to define global styles separately when using styled-components. This can help you maintain a consistent look and feel across your application while still keeping your component styles modular. You can define global styles by creating a separate styled-components file, such as `_globals.css`, and importing it into your application.

For example, you could create a `_globals.css` file with the following contents:

```css
body {
  font-family: 'Arial', sans-serif;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  background-color: #f4f4f4;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

button {
  background-color: #007bff;
  color: white;
  border: none;
  padding: 10px;
  font-size: 16px;
  cursor: pointer;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0056b3;
}
```

Then, you can import this file into your application:

```javascript
import './_globals.css';
```

This way, you can define global styles in a separate file while still using styled-components to style individual components.
